  "notes": "As East has overcalled in spades, West's ♠Q lead is certain to be a singleton. If South makes the natural looking play of covering with dummy's ♠K at trick 1, the contract will fail.\n\nEast will win the ♠A and play spades, allowing West to ruff out the ♠J. South has lost 2 tricks and still has a spade loser. The contract will depend on the diamond finesse. That finesse will probably lose as East rates to have the ♦K for his overcall.\n\nIf South plays low from dummy at trick 1, the defense has no recourse. If East ducks, West may switch to diamonds, but South is in control. South takes the ♦A and draws trump, eventually surrendering tricks to the ♠A and ♦K. Dummy will have a diamond winner to take care of South's club loser.\n\nIf East had taken his ♠A at trick 1 and given West a ruff, South would have 2 spade tricks and 10 top tricks. South should win the diamond shift, draw trump, and cash his 10 tricks.",
